WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.989 --> 00:00:04.580
If you've watched my review of Disney's new
live-action Beauty and the Beast movie, you'll

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.200
know how much I enjoyed it.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.269
But now I want to see how much of a super-fan
you are of the original animated movie, which,

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:14.589
by the way, was the first animated feature
ever to be nominated for a Best Picture Oscar.

00:00:14.589 --> 00:00:18.910
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video, I'm going to reveal a wealth

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.439
of fascinating facts about Disney's classic
animation, Beauty and the Beast, so be my

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:26.260
guest and put your Disney knowledge to the
test!

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:30.300
When it came to creating the book-loving Belle,
the filmmakers took their cues from the golden

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.560
age of Hollywood.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.720
For example, Belle's blue-and-white outfit
channels actress Judy Garland's look in the

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.110
Wizard of Oz, as well as emphasising visually
just how different Belle is from the people

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:44.790
in her town as she's the only character who
wears blue in the opening sequence.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.620
But Belle's costume isn't the only thing that
links her to Judy Garland.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.890
In fact, one of the reasons that Paige O'Hara
was selected to bring Belle to life was that

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.690
her voice and manner were reminiscent of the
quality and warmth of Garland's voice.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.500
Other Hollywood icons also left their mark
on the character of Belle.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.549
For instance, screenwriter Linda Woolverton
took cues from actress Katharine Hepburn,

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:10.490
especially her performance as the spirited
and literature-loving Jo March in the 1930s

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:11.600
film Little Women.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.600
And that was just the beginning of screen
legend Hepburn's influence on the animated

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.600
classic.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.670
Indeed, Belle's interaction with the Beast,
for example, when she tends his wounds, was

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.969
partly inspired by the on-screen verbal sparring
between Hepburn and Spencer Tracy in the many

00:01:24.969 --> 00:01:27.090
movies they made together.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.189
As we all know from his boastful song, no
one does anything quite like Gaston.

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:36.400
Yet, despite Gaston's insistence on his uniqueness,
the filmmakers did actually draw inspiration

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.920
from a number of places when they created
his character.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.399
First of all, they channelled the pompous
Roman soldier, Miles Gloriosus, from Stephen

00:01:43.399 --> 00:01:47.479
Sondheim's musical, A Funny Thing Happened
on the Way to the Forum, which was made into

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:51.909
a film in 1966, and was a particular favourite
of Beauty and the Beast producer Don Hahn

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:53.999
and lyricist Howard Ashman.

00:01:53.999 --> 00:01:58.299
And when she was writing the big-headed Gaston,
screenwriter Linda Woolverton also had in

00:01:58.299 --> 00:01:59.530
mind some of her ex-boyfriends.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:05.350
Looks-wise, the animators modelled Gaston
after good looking soap opera stars and added

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.860
a dash of Superman, as the idea was to make
him incredibly handsome and, basically the

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.000
polar opposite of the Beast in both personality
and appearance.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.030
Speaking of the Beast, animator Glen Keane,
who designed that character, was banned from

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.250
meeting actor Robby Benson who voiced the
Beast until after the movie was finished,

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:26.420
which was very unusual because, on other animated
movies, Keane normally spent time with the

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.600
actors during their recording sessions.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:33.030
However, Jeffrey Katzenberg, who was Disney's
studio chief at the time, specifically didn't

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:37.720
want Keane to be influenced by Benson's looks,
personality, or mannerisms in how he animated

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:38.820
the Beast.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.590
As for the design of the Beast, that was based
on a variety of animals that Keane observed

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:46.900
and sketched at London Zoo in Regent's Park,
including wolves, gorillas, and mandrills,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.430
whose eyes he felt were very human and conveyed
real intelligence.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.870
Keane also bought a buffalo head from a taxidermist.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.850
And in the end, the features of the Beast
combine a lion's mane, a buffalo's head, a

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:03.700
gorilla's brow, human eyes, a cow's ears,
a bear's body, and a wolf's tail and legs.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:08.100
When making animated movies, more often than
not, actors record their roles separately,

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.010
but for Beauty and the Beast, Paige O'Hara
persuaded Disney's powers that be to let her

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:16.210
and Robby Benson record Belle and Beast's
lines together, which they found really helped

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:21.320
them get the dynamic right between their characters
as it developed over the course of the story.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:25.090
By the way, Benson prepared for his Beauty
and the Beast recording sessions by doing

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:29.400
the kind of vocal warm-ups he used to get
ready when he performed in Broadway shows.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.870
It's almost hard to imagine now that we know
the movie so well, but several of the film's

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.220
characters started out with different names.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.440
In earlier versions of the story, Mrs Potts
was actually called Mrs Chamomile.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.960
The filmmakers originally named her 'Chamomile'
after the soothing herbal tea, but decided

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.780
to change it when it occurred to them it may
be difficult to pronounce, especially for

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.780
young children.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:54.130
As for Jerry Orbach's character Lumiere, he
was initially going to be named Chandal, short

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:58.710
for the word chandelier, but again, the filmmakers
thought the name would be too tough to say,

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:02.430
so instead they went with the name we know
today, which was inspired by early cinema

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.870
pioneers the Lumiere Brothers.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:09.620
Mrs Potts may sound wonderfully soothing singing
about the 'tale as old as time' in the movie's

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.680
title track, however, what happened to actress
Angela Lansbury right before she recorded

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.530
the song was anything but!

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:19.099
Lansbury was on her way to record the tune
with the New York Philharmonic when the plane

00:04:19.099 --> 00:04:23.139
she was on was forced to make an emergency
landing in Las Vegas due to a bomb scare!

00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:28.370
Although producer Don Hahn suggested she rest
up overnight in Vegas, undeterred, Lansbury

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:32.820
caught another flight and continued on her
journey to New York that same day, going straight

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:37.180
into her recording session with the Philharmonic,
where she nailed the song in just one take,

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.780
which is exactly what we hear in the film!

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:45.169
Originally, Mrs Potts' son Chip was only supposed
to say a single sentence in the whole movie,

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:47.960
and that line was: 'Momma, there's a girl
in the castle'.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:51.550
The rest of the scenes where Chip appears
in the film as it stands today were actually

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:55.770
meant to feature a Music Box which, instead
of speaking, made little chiming noises.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:59.860
However, the filmmakers decided they wanted
to add a child's perspective to the movie

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:03.960
in order to make it easier for kids to relate
to the story, and because they liked child

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.830
actor Bradley Michael Pierce's voice as Chip,
they kept increasing his dialogue.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:12.580
One of the most enchanting parts of Beauty
and the Beast is its songs, so it's incredible

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:17.240
to think that composer Alan Menken and lyricist
Howard Ashman ever doubted their brilliance

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:18.389
for even a second!

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:22.050
In fact, they were so uncertain whether the
filmmakers would like the opening song they'd

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:26.189
written for Belle that they flew from Upstate
New York, where they wrote the soundtrack,

00:05:26.189 --> 00:05:29.669
all the way to Los Angeles to play it in person
for the filmmaking team.

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:33.249
Basically, Menken and Ashman were worried
the filmmakers would think they'd gone mad

00:05:33.249 --> 00:05:37.520
because they covered so many details in the
space of just a few minutes in that first

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.970
song; so, for example, we meet Belle, Gaston
and LeFou, and we find out what kind of people

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:47.249
they are, and we learn all about Belle's town
and its inhabitants and how they're so different

00:05:47.249 --> 00:05:48.249
from her.

00:05:48.249 --> 00:05:51.520
But Menken and Ashman shouldn't have worried
as the Beauty and the Beast crew loved the

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.469
song and the entertaining way it presented
so much information and set up many of the

00:05:55.469 --> 00:05:57.090
film's main characters.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:01.540
And, in fact, the song "Belle" actually ended
up being one of a small number of tracks that

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:05.629
didn't get changed or rearranged over the
course of the movie's development.

